From: Mirdispo contributors
Subject: Capture the sound in larger periods

The receiver's sound stuttered. pulsesrc asks for 10 ms periods by default,
so the sound server drives the graph around the stream's null sink in
periods of 256 samples. Neither the audio grabber nor the players feeding
the sink kept up with that: both recorded underruns by the hundred within
minutes, each one a gap in what the receiver plays.

The grabber now asks for 40 ms periods and a 400 ms buffer, which lets the
graph run at the period players already ask for. That is small next to the
latency of the stream itself.

diff -ruN a/src/nd-pulseaudio.c b/src/nd-pulseaudio.c
--- a/src/nd-pulseaudio.c
+++ b/src/nd-pulseaudio.c
@@ -584,10 +584,18 @@
 
   g_autofree gchar *device_name = g_strdup_printf (ND_PA_SINK "_%.8s.monitor", self->uuid);
   g_autofree gchar *client_name = g_strdup_printf ("GNOME Network Displays Audio Grabber for %s", self->name);
+  /* With pulsesrc's default 10 ms period the sound server runs the whole
+   * graph around the sink in periods of 256 samples, which neither this
+   * source nor the players feeding the sink keep up with: both record
+   * underruns, heard as a stutter on the receiver. A 40 ms period lets the
+   * graph run at the period players already ask for, and costs nothing
+   * next to the latency of the stream itself. */
   g_object_set (src,
                 "device", device_name,
                 "client-name", client_name,
                 "do-timestamp", TRUE,
+                "latency-time", (gint64) 40000,
+                "buffer-time", (gint64) 400000,
                 "server", pa_context_get_server (self->context),
                 NULL);
 
